      "text": "An architect accepts a commission while not registered/licensed and not holding a temporary/special permit. Which source pattern is safest?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "Unauthorized practice under RA 9266 Section 29"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "A harmless renewal delay"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "A market discount"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "An arbitration clause"
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "a",
      "correct_value": null,
      "accepted_answers": null,
      "value_tolerance": 0,
      "question_type": "mcq",
      "rationale": {
        "correct": "Section 29 covers practicing or offering to practice without registration/licensure or permit.",
        "takeaway": "Section 29 covers practicing or offering to practice without registration/licensure or permit."
      },
      "explanation": "The timing of renewal is irrelevant. The violation occurs at the moment of accepting the work without a valid license. [cite: 540] Fraud requires intentional misrepresentation. While practicing without a license is wrong, it does not necessarily constitute fraud unless the architect affirmatively misrepresented having a valid license. [cite: 538] Negligence in timing mischaracterizes the violation. This is a regulatory violation for practicing architecture without a current license—a categorical prohibition. [cite: 540] Practicing without a current license is a regulatory violation; subsequent renewal does not cure the period of illegal practice. [cite: 540, 542] Practicing without current license is a non-negotiable violation. [cite: 542]",
      "code_citation": "RA 9266 Section 34",
